post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  Loom configuration installed.

  This package deliberately does not enable services or rewrite your
  bootloader: doing that from a package install would be rude. Finish with:

    sudo /usr/share/loom-payload/scripts/bootstrap.sh --user <you>

  That script is idempotent, so it is also the right thing to run after an
  upgrade that adds a new service.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  if [ -f /etc/kernel/cmdline ]; then
    cat <<'MSG'

  The kernel images were rebuilt and re-signed by the pacman hooks. If this
  upgrade touched anything under /etc/initcpio, confirm the rescue entry still
  exists before you need it:

    loomctl health

MSG
  fi
}

pre_remove() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  Removing loom-base leaves /etc/mkinitcpio.d/linux.preset pointing at
  /efi/EFI/loom. Kernel images keep building, but the rescue entry will no
  longer be rebuilt on kernel upgrades, and a stale rescue image cannot read a
  newer kernel's disks.

MSG
}
